// healing domain -- the self-healing two-tier base/overlay store (Epic 4
// #2077, E4.2 / memql#2140).
//
// THE TWO-TIER MODEL, in one paragraph. A construct that the self-healing
// loop can repair (an automation, a precondition, a guard, a literal) has
// TWO tiers of definition. The BASE tier is the immutable, authored,
// embedded definition -- the .memql the engine ships and re-materializes on
// every boot; it is the deploy spine's source of truth and is NEVER
// LLM-healed. The OVERLAY tier is a healed OVERRIDE: a data row produced by
// the repair loop (E4.4), human-validated (E4.5), that shadows its base
// construct when -- and only when -- it is VALID. Resolution prefers the
// most-recent VALID overlay override for a base construct and falls back to
// the base otherwise. This mirrors the Epic 1 RBAC base/custom hybrid:
// immutable `predefined` base roles authored in DSL + dynamic custom roles
// added as data, resolved newest-wins (dsl/rbac/concepts.memql:role,
// component/memql/rbac_role_immutable_validation.go).
//
// WHY a `tier` field, not just "base rows are embedded". The base tier IS
// the embedded .memql construct -- there is normally no base-tier healing
// ROW. But the immutability axis must still be representable + guarded so
// that (a) a deploy-authored, never-heal override can be pinned as
// tier=base data when a pack ships a canonical healed form, and (b) a
// non-system actor can NEVER forge a tier=base row to bypass the override-
// is-data contract. The validateHealingBaseImmutable Go guard
// (component/memql/healing_base_immutable_validation.go) rejects any
// non-system-actor write to a tier=base row, exactly as
// validateRbacBaseRoleImmutable does for predefined roles.
//
// Per-row authz tier: owned. A healed override is owned by the user whose
// construct was healed; every read gates on ownerUserId==actor.userId and
// every write stamps ownerUserId from actor.userId server-side. The repair
// loop + validation flow run as that user (or a system actor for embedded
// base materialization). Multi-node consistent: overrides live in the
// shared Postgres and any node resolves the same newest-valid override; a
// graph write to the concept rides the cache.invalidate broadcast so a
// cached resolution is dropped on every replica.

use identity.concepts.{ user }

/// A two-tier definition of a self-healable construct: the immutable BASE (authored/embedded, never
/// LLM-healed -- the deploy spine's source of truth) and a healed OVERLAY override (data, produced
/// by the repair loop, human-validated) that shadows its base when valid. Resolution prefers the
/// most-recent VALID overlay override for a baseConstructId and falls back to the base. Mirrors the
/// Epic 1 RBAC base/custom hybrid (immutable predefined base + dynamic custom data, newest-wins).
/// The `tier` field is the immutability axis: a tier=base row is immutable at runtime (the
/// validateHealingBaseImmutable Go guard rejects any non-system-actor write to it, so an override
/// can never be forged as base to escape the override-is-data contract); a tier=overlay row is the
/// healed data an actor may add. Per-row authz: owned -- ownerUserId is stamped from actor.userId
/// on every write and every read gates on ownerUserId==actor.userId. Multi-node consistent via the
/// shared Postgres + the cache.invalidate broadcast.
@rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId")
concept healedOverride {
  ownerUserId      string!  @description("v1:identity:user.id that owns this override -- the user whose construct was healed. Stamped from actor.userId at create time and re-stamped on every update so ownership can never be reassigned by a caller. The load-bearing per-row authz key. For an embedded tier=base row materialized by the SeedMaterializer this is the system owner.")
  baseConstructId  string!  @description("Stable id of the BASE construct this row defines or overrides -- the automation name, precondition id, guard id, or literal key the healing loop targets (e.g. 'deployStaging' or 'deployStaging.envIsStaging'). The resolution key: resolveConstruct(baseConstructId) prefers the newest VALID overlay override with this baseConstructId and falls back to the base. Mirrors how v1:rbac:capability.roleSlug + role.slug join a grant to its role by a stable string, not a node id.")
  tier             enum("base", "overlay")!  @default("overlay")  @description("The immutability axis. base = the immutable, authored/embedded definition -- the deploy spine's source of truth, never LLM-healed; a tier=base row is materialized only by a system actor (the SeedMaterializer) and the validateHealingBaseImmutable Go guard rejects any non-system-actor write to it. overlay = a healed override produced by the repair loop (E4.4) + human-validated (E4.5); it is editable data an actor may add and is the default for any runtime write. Resolution prefers a VALID overlay over base.")
  overrideData     object  @description("The healed construct body -- the typed-patch RESULT (E4.3): the construct definition (e.g. the rewritten automation / precondition / guard / literal) that this override resolves to. Empty for a placeholder/pending row. Named overrideData (not the reserved `payload` intrinsic). The two-tier resolver returns this verbatim when this override wins resolution.")
  valid            bool    @default("false")  @description("Whether this override is a VALID, resolution-eligible definition. Only a valid override shadows its base -- the core of the two-tier contract: resolution prefers a VALID overlay override and otherwise falls back to base. A proposed-but-not-yet-validated override (E4.5 human validation pending) carries valid=false and is invisible to resolution, so an unvalidated heal can never silently take effect. Set true on human acceptance (E4.5).")
  version          int     @description("Monotonic version of this override for a given baseConstructId. Always written: proposeOverride takes `version int!` as a required arg and accepts it, so there is no omitted case. each accepted heal captures a NEW version (E4.5 capture-as-version) rather than mutating the prior one, so the override history is an audit trail and a pin can resolve an exact version. Resolution prefers the highest-version VALID overlay; ties break on newest createdAt.")
  active           bool    @default("true")  @description("Soft-deactivate flag. An inactive override is ignored at resolution time but kept for audit -- lets a healed override be retired (reverting resolution to base or an older valid override) without churning row history. Base rows are never deactivated (the immutability guard blocks it).")
  reason           string  @description("Human-readable note explaining WHY this override exists -- the precondition-miss that triggered the heal, the patch kind applied, the validator's comment. Surfaced in the cockpit healed-pack view (E4.6) for auditability. Optional.")
  sourcePreconditionId  string  @description("Optional back-pointer to the precondition (E4.1) whose miss triggered this heal -- the healing.precondition.missed signal's preconditionId. Empty for a manually-authored override. Lets the cockpit group healed overrides under the precondition-miss that produced them and lets the repair loop reconcile what it has already proposed for a given miss.")
  blastRadius      enum("personal", "shared", "spine_adjacent")  @default("personal")  @description("How widely this heal propagates -- the validation effort axis (E4.5 / memql#2143). Bigger blast radius => a HIGHER minimum role rank is required to validate it (the validateHealingValidationRankBound Go guard gates the valid false->true transition on actor.rank >= the radius-required rank). personal = the heal only affects the owner's own construct (min rank: user); shared = the heal affects a construct other principals depend on (min rank: admin); spine_adjacent = the heal sits next to the deploy spine / cross-cutting infrastructure (min rank: developer). Owner can validate any radius. Mirrors Epic 1's rank model (owner > developer > admin > user).")
  validationStatus enum("proposed", "validated", "rejected")  @default("proposed")  @description("Human-validation state (E4.5). proposed = the repair loop proposed it (valid=false, invisible to resolution); validated = a role-appropriate human accepted it (flips valid=true, captured as a new version); rejected = a human declined it (valid stays false, kept for audit). The validation flow transitions proposed -> validated|rejected; resolution only ever returns validated+valid+active overlays.")
  validatedBy      string  @description("v1:identity:user.id of the human who validated (or rejected) this override. Stamped from actor.userId by the validate/reject mutation. Empty while proposed. The audit trail for who accepted a heal at what blast radius.")
  validatedAt      datetime  @description("RFC3339 timestamp of the validation/rejection decision. Empty while proposed.")
  rejectionReason  string  @description("Human-readable reason a validator REJECTED this override. Empty unless validationStatus=rejected. Kept for audit so a rejected proposal is recorded, not silently dropped.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="ownerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
}
